     \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 counters B2's gift distinction; A5 explicitly follows B4's switch from cake to bread.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 asserts that the mixer was unconditional and B4 announces an independent change in the bakery's direction.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 insists the supposed investment must participate even after B4 abandons the cake premise.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a bakery mixer and menu decision; B2 adds a personal birthday relationship.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's 'particular vision of cake' and A5's meeting with bread extend the same investment logic economically.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"I bought your bakery mixer. Save me a place at the menu tasting.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"It was a birthday gift, not a share in the bakery.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"I backed your cakes. I should taste what my support produces.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"We're switching to bread. Come Friday; the customers will choose.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"I'll come Thursday. They should only choose between loaves I've approved.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A converts a birthday gift into an enduring right to influence the bakery, then promotes that right from consultation to prior approval.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Enforcing actual investor rights.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"B2 explicitly denies that the gift bought a share; A3 still claims influence through support, and A5 claims approval over the customers' bread choices.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a turns an invitation to taste a changed product into a menu veto; b uses repaid support to demand access to earlier naming decisions. Both preserve past help as continuing influence.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The rejected candidate ended with a figurative meeting rather than an actionable claim. The revision keeps the mixer, birthday gift and switch from cake to bread, adding a customer tasting. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, because A3/A5 derive continuing rights from the gift despite B2; logic 5, because B2 prompts the support claim and B4's Friday customer choice prompts Thursday approval; relationship 4, because B2 defends ownership and B4 includes A without granting control; heightening 4, because A5 promotes tasting access into screening authority; specificity 5, because A1's tasting and B4's date establish practical choices; brevity/wit 4, because A5's earlier visit concretely reveals the expanded entitlement. Total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns, under 75 words; all dialogue gates pass. Original firstPass remains unchanged.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 states the veto openly; it remains an attempted exercise of influence that B can contest.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S56-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"0ec6a49aae917a8da0f4aee4629245c7c5a8c6926fd4f650154ecfd608f233ce\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 invokes the deposit to influence the club name; A3 keeps the claim alive after B2 confirms repayment.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 distinguishes financial repayment from ongoing confidence; A5 answers B4's question about when the obligation ends.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 raises a strong repayment objection, but B4 becomes a broad question rather than pursuing the club's naming decision.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 expands the claim indefinitely but supplies no concrete next exercise of influence.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a club renaming and an old deposit, though A3–A5 move away from the actual name.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Financially' is concise; A5's 'decisions remain available' is an abstract formulation of the premise.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 80\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Before changing the club name, remember who paid the deposit.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"We've paid you back.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"The money, yes. I still backed the club we're naming.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"The printer needs a name today. Pick between these two.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Let me see the rejected names too. My support deserves more than the final round.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats an old contribution as a continuing right to influence decisions even after its financial repayment.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Collecting an unpaid debt.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 accepts B2's repayment as complete but retains influence; A5 demands broader naming participation on the strength of past support.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b bargains over the extent of consultation after repayment; a claims influence over a birthday gift recipient's new product direction.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original ended with a general statement of endless entitlement. The revision preserves the club-name and deposit setting. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity/wit 4 = 92. B4 offers limited participation under a printer deadline; A5 treats that concession as grounds to reopen earlier choices. Five spoken ABABA turns; under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 risks delaying the printing, giving B a useful continuing objection without determining the scene's ending.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S57-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"10f0ade0ec7d5443fb44d0b258b92d4c7f4e3377f3136bc54968216c4e9f6671\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 prepares the director's options and A3 presents steering as assistance; A's second-in-command rank is implied rather than explicit.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 reframes B2's steering accusation; A5 renames the option specifically to exploit B4's alphabetical-order requirement.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 notices the manipulation and B4 imposes a credible neutral sorting method that changes A's tactic.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 moves from visual emphasis to altering the option's name while apparently complying with B4.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a director's decision pack, larger print and option ordering as immediately playable details.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 5,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5's 'A Sensible Beginning' simultaneously obeys alphabetical sorting and preserves the recommendation's privileged position.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"As assistant director, I've costed both sets. The garden fits our budget.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"So does my kitchen. Give the director both designs.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Certainly. I'll put the garden sketch on the cover to brighten the pack.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"Use a plain cover. We can each present our design.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"I'll schedule you both. The garden designer can go first while you're setting up.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A performs the deputy's useful preparation work while shifting presentation and scheduling details to favor a preferred decision.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Openly campaigning for a preferred option.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 establishes assistant-director responsibility and completed costing; A3 presents priority as an attractive cover, and A5 implements B4's presentations while giving the favored design first access.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a manages a theater decision pack and presentation schedule in conversation with a competing designer; b handles an urgent harbor report and frames a messenger's introduction.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The rejected candidate had a clear ordering trick but little deputy relationship or decision context. The new illustrative setting is a theater set choice, retaining the director's options and biased presentation. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, because A1 supplies reliable deputy work and A3/A5 quietly steer its delivery; logic 5, because B2's inclusion request prompts cover bias and B4's plain-cover remedy moves A's influence into scheduling; relationship 4, because B2 has a competing design and B4 proposes equal presentation; heightening 4, because A5 shifts from paperwork prominence to first access; specificity 5, because A1/B2 establish costed garden and kitchen sets; brevity/wit 3, because A5's setup rationale is playable but less sharply comic than the original alphabetic title. Total 90. Five spoken ABABA turns, under 75 words; all dialogue gates pass. Original firstPass remains unchanged.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's scheduling advantage is subtle, and B may reasonably challenge why only the kitchen designer needs setup time.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S57-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"73fe1bbfbd08b306c887d4b16ce824065c59d65c1483e0551004fe5a7f9ac8d1\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 controls the captain's messages, but A3 casually suppresses route-changing information, weakening the reliable-deputy aspect.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 classifies B2's route message; A5 yields to B4's insistence on informing the captain while adding a preferred route.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 credibly seek delivery but give little detail about urgency or the route decision.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 changes suppression into framing, yet states the preferred-route manipulation openly rather than developing a convincing service tactic.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a captain and messages, but B2's route change remains unspecified.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3's 'needn't worry about' provides a recognizable dodge; A5 mostly explains A's manipulation.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 64\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"The captain's finishing her briefing. As first mate, I'm collecting harbor updates.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"North harbor's closed. South and west both have berths available.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"I'll flag the closure immediately and attach my recommendation for west.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"I'll report the options myself. I haven't recommended either.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Certainly. I'll introduce you and bring both berth sheets. West's booking form is already filled in; I'll leave it beside her pen.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A reliably supplies information and access while using advance paperwork and its placement to make the preferred decision easier to execute.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Misrepresenting a colleague's recommendation.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 promptly relays B2's closure and attributes the west recommendation to A; after B4 insists on neutrality, A5 preserves both berth sheets and B's direct report while privileging west through a completed form beside the captain's pen.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"The frozen theater alternative favors a competing design through cover prominence and presentation order; this harbor alternative accommodates a neutral messenger's direct access while making A's preferred booking ready for signature. The frozen a record retains its earlier comparison as required.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The rejected candidate's A5 contradicted B4's neutrality by announcing the report as support for west, weakening the reliable-deputy behavior. This correction keeps the harbor setting and A1 through B4, replacing that attribution with useful but selective preparation. Separate internal reread under coach-1.0: card fidelity 5, because A1/A3 perform first-mate duties and A5 supplies both options while quietly favoring one through paperwork; response logic 5, because B2's closure prompts A3's immediate alert and B4's direct-report request shifts A5 from attaching a recommendation to arranging access and a ready booking; relationship 4, because B4 protects the messenger's neutrality and secures direct delivery; heightening 4, because A5 moves from recommending west to putting its execution beside the captain's pen; specificity 4, because A1/B2 establish the briefing and available harbors and A5 adds a playable form, although west's merits remain unstated; brevity/wit 4, because A5's completed form and pen placement reveal the preference through helpful administrative work. Weighted total 90. Five spoken ABABA turns, 65 words; all dialogue gates pass. The captain's decision remains open. Original firstPass is preserved exactly; this is an internal textual reread, with no runtime or live testing.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's advantage is convenience, not a guaranteed choice, and A's reason for preferring west remains unspecified. The comedy is restrained; the selective preparation must read as sincere helpfulness.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S58-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"5edf00c44721b3c701b1e7ad806437070f24ca11b58fb01c0286bfdecfb677a3\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1 treats a kettle repair as managerial evidence; A3 keeps preparing, but the contest has no visible comparator.\"\n          },\n          \"responseLogic\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A3 turns B2's lack of vacancy into time for another repair; A5 answers B4's campaign objection by renaming the activity.\"\n          },\n          \"relationship\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"B2 corrects the vacancy assumption and B4 labels campaigning without presenting a fresh practical choice.\"\n          },\n          \"heightening\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5's 'succession appliances' restates the leadership interpretation without adding evidence or consequence.\"\n          },\n          \"specificity\": {\n            \"score\": 4,\n            \"evidence\": \"A1's office kettle and A3's toaster make the small achievements concrete.\"\n          },\n          \"brevityWit\": {\n            \"score\": 3,\n            \"evidence\": \"A5 coins 'succession appliances,' but the phrase substitutes for another action in the imagined contest.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 72\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"I fixed the office kettle. Put that in my column for next manager.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"There's no vacancy. Sam bought the replacement fuse.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Give Sam procurement. Give me delivery.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"B\",\n          \"text\": \"You two could fix the toaster together.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"speaker\": \"A\",\n          \"text\": \"Excellent. I'll coordinate Sam. We can finally compare technical ability with leadership.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"decision\":